# Browserslist targets for eslint-plugin-compat.
#
# THIS FILE EXISTS BECAUSE `compat: true` IS MEANINGLESS WITHOUT IT.
# eslint-plugin-compat checks API usage against BROWSERSLIST targets, and with
# no browserslist config it falls back to browserslist's default query, which
# includes `op_mini all` — Opera Mini, which supports almost nothing. The plugin
# then reports `Promise` and `URL` as unsupported and the lint run becomes noise
# somebody switches off. That is exactly why eslint.config.js carried
# `compat: false` from Phase 1 until a frontend existed.
#
# THE TARGET, AND HOW IT WAS ARRIVED AT.
# DefMiner's frontend runs in ONE renderer: the Electron renderer inside the
# Caido desktop app. It is never served to a browser, so the usual "what do our
# users run" question has a single measurable answer.
#
# MEASURED: Caido 0.58.2 on this machine reports Chrome/148.0.7778.180 —
# read out of /Applications/Caido.app/Contents/Frameworks/Electron
# Framework.framework this session, not inferred from a release table.
#
# The target below is set BELOW that measurement on purpose, and the direction
# matters. A target lower than reality is STRICTER: the plugin flags more APIs,
# so the cost of being wrong is a lint fix. A target higher than reality is
# PERMISSIVE: it silently allows an API the operator's renderer does not have,
# and the cost is a broken page on their machine. 140 leaves roughly two
# Electron majors of headroom for an operator on an older Caido — Phase 0
# measured against 0.57.1, which is not installed here and so was not measured —
# while staying far above the point where anything this codebase uses
# (Promise, URL, structuredClone, Array.prototype.at) became baseline.
#
# Revisit when a Caido release raises the floor and somebody MEASURES the new
# one. Do not raise it from a changelog.
chrome >= 140
